{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of each lottery based on the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. Skewness here is defined as a greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. For Lottery A (2400 Euros with 100% probability), all probability is concentrated on a single, fixed outcome—no variability, hence no skewness in the defined sense. For Lottery B, there is a 33% chance of 2500 (slightly better than A), 66% chance of 2400 (same as A), and 1% chance of 0 (much worse). The unfavorable outcome (0) has a very low probability (1%), while the favorable outcome (2500) has a moderate probability (33%). The majority of probability (66%) is on the outcome equal to A, and only 1% on the worst outcome. Therefore, Lottery B is not right-skewed under the given definition, since the greater portion of probability is not assigned to unfavorable outcomes. In fact, the distribution is left-skewed or nearly symmetric. The reason, which prefers more right-skewed lotteries, would not favor B over A. However, the reason is still applicable in principle, as skewness can be assessed from the given outcomes and probabilities.",
  "step_2": "The individual considered the probability of achieving at least the same outcome as Lottery A (2400 Euros). In Lottery B, there is a 99% chance (33% + 66%) of getting either 2500 or 2400, both of which are at least as good as A’s 2400. The individual notes that this high probability (99%) of not losing out is a key factor. The individual also expresses a willingness to take the risk (the 1% chance of getting 0 Euros), indicating a preference for the potential gain (2500) despite the small downside. The justification centers on the high probability of not doing worse than A and the acceptance of a small risk for a possible gain. No mention is made of skewness, distribution of probability across outcomes in terms of 'favorable vs. unfavorable', or any reference to the shape of the distribution. The focus is on comparative likelihood of not losing, not on the skewness of the lottery.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness of the lotteries, specifically whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes. The individual did not consider skewness at all. Instead, they focused on the high probability (99%) of achieving at least the same outcome as A, and the willingness to accept a small risk. This reasoning is based on stochastic dominance (first-order) and risk tolerance, not on the skewness of the distribution. The information considered by the individual (99% chance of not losing) is not directly related to the skewness criterion. The justification does not align with the rule of the decision reason, which prefers more right-skewed lotteries. Since the individual did not mention or use any concept related to skewness, and their reasoning is based on a different criterion (probability of not being worse off), the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
